  "query_preview": "(Tuesday through Sunday):\n\nMain forecast challenges will be in Tuesday-Thursday portion of\nthe forecast, as a cold front becomes stationary over central\nIllinois. Another surge of heat will take place Tuesday with\nhighs in the 90s over much of the forecast area, lingering south\nof the boundary Wednesday-Thursday. Much of the guidance has\nthis front somewhere between I-72 and I-70, and any waffling of\nthe front may result in forecast temperature busts. Box and \nwhiskers plots of the NBM members show about a 10-12 degree\nhigh temperature spread along I-72 for the 25-75th percentiles \nWednesday and Thursday, widening further next weekend as upper\nlevel high pressure builds across the Mississippi Valley.\nPotential for a heat advisory on Tuesday will need to be watched\nclosely, but concerns for clouds/rain Tuesday preclude issuing\none this far in advance.\n\nPeriods of showers and thunderstorms will occur during this \nsame time span, especially near and north of the boundary. \nPrecipitable water values near the top end of mid-September \nclimatology continue to result in concerns for heavy rain. \nMorning guidance has continued to focus the heaviest rain out \nacross Iowa and northwest Missouri, with probabilities of more \nthan 2 inches of rain around 50% as close as Galesburg. Chances \nfor more than an inch are around 50-70% near Galesburg and \nPeoria (per morning NBM), dropping to around 20% near I-72. The\nheat on Tuesday should help keep a good part of the day quiet,\nthough the cap may erode enough by late afternoon to get strong\nto locally severe storms going. Latest SPC Day3 outlook has a\nlevel 1 severe risk over most of the area, which seems\nreasonable.\n\nLate in the week, as the ridge builds northward, the front will\nlift back north with it, with some indications of it returning\nlater in the weekend. Blended guidance keeps 20-40% PoP's going\nwith the uncertainty in timing and placement.",
